## Statement

Assume AC. Let $\mathcal P_0$ be a family of probability laws and let
$M=(M_t)_{t\in\mathbb N_0}$ be a nonnegative test supermartingale for
$\mathcal P_0$. For every $P\in\mathcal P_0$, every $a>0$, and every
$N\in\mathbb N_0$,
$$
P\!\left(\max_{0\le t\le N}M_t\ge a\right)\le \frac{\mathbb E_PM_0}{a}\le\frac1a.
$$

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Assume AC. Let $\mathcal P_0$ be a null family and let
$M=(M_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a nonnegative test supermartingale for that family. Fix
$P\in\mathcal P_0$, $a>0$, and a finite horizon $N\in\mathbb N_0$.

[F1] [[def-nonnegative-test-supermartingale]]: under every null law, $M$ is adapted and integrable, is nonnegative almost surely at every time, is a supermartingale, and satisfies $\mathbb E_PM_0\le1$.

[F2] [[lem-first-hitting-time-of-an-adapted-process-is-a-stopping-time]]: for an adapted process and a Borel set $B$, its first time in $B$ is a stopping time.

[F3] [[def-discrete-stopping-time]]: a discrete stopping time is a map into $\mathbb N_0\cup\{\infty\}$ whose events $\{\tau\le n\}$ belong to $\mathcal F_n$; it is bounded when it is bounded almost surely by a deterministic integer.

[F4] [[thm-optional-sampling-for-bounded-stopping-times]]: for bounded stopping times $\sigma\le\tau$, optional sampling for a supermartingale gives $\mathbb E_PM_\tau\le\mathbb E_PM_\sigma$.

[F5] [[cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound]] and [[cor-expectation-of-an-indicator-is-probability]]: expectation is monotone, and $\mathbb E_P[\mathbf1_A]=P(A)$ for measurable $A$.

[A1] AC is assumed by the test-supermartingale definition and the bounded optional-sampling theorem ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]); it is used only through those suppliers.

## Proof

1.1 Define $\tau_a=\inf\{t\ge0:M_t\ge a\}$, with $\inf\varnothing=\infty$, and $A_N=\{\tau_a\le N\}=\{\max_{0\le t\le N}M_t\ge a\}$. By [F1], $M$ is adapted; applying [F2] with the Borel set $[a,\infty)$ makes $\tau_a$ a stopping time. Thus $\tau_a\wedge N$ and the constant time $0$ are bounded stopping times, with $0\le\tau_a\wedge N$. [given, F1, F2, F3, A1, choose]

2.1 Apply [F4] to $0\le\tau_a\wedge N$ to get $\mathbb E_PM_{\tau_a\wedge N}\le\mathbb E_PM_0\le1$. [F1, F4, A1, step 1.1]

2.2 On $A_N$, $\tau_a\wedge N=\tau_a$ and $M_{\tau_a}\ge a$; on $A_N^c$, $\tau_a\wedge N=N$ and $M_N\ge0$ almost surely by [F1]. Hence $M_{\tau_a\wedge N}\ge a\mathbf1_{A_N}$ almost surely. [F1, step 1.1]

3.1 By [F5] and step 2.1, $aP(A_N)=\mathbb E_P[a\mathbf1_{A_N}]\le\mathbb E_PM_{\tau_a\wedge N}\le\mathbb E_PM_0\le1$. Dividing by $a>0$ proves both bounds. [F5, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra] ∎

## Remarks

The finite-horizon event is exactly the first-crossing event by time $N$. No assumption that an infinite-horizon supremum is attained is used here.
